{
  "raw": {
    "https://cobleskill.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205110": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar",
      "description": "SUNY Cobleskill\nJob Posting: Assistant Registrar SUNY Cobleskill \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Registrar Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Registrar Category: Staff Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Registrar Locations: Cobleskill, NY Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Closes: Oct 8, 2026 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Full-time Position ID: 205110 Share About SUNY Cobleskill: As New York's leading public college for agriculture and technology, SUNY Cobleskill Ag & Tech develops students who wish to positively impact the future well-being of human society and sustainable practices. Our immersive approach to learning combines academic rigor with hands-on application, producing graduates who enter their careers with proven real-world capabilities. Students thrive on our 900-acre campus, providing a dynamic rural setting with a working farm, modern laboratories, and natural ecosystems serving as our classrooms. As members of this close-knit community, students gain a sense of belonging and support for lifelong learning and professional growth through direct interaction with expert faculty and industry leaders. An anchor institution in the Mohawk Valley region, the College promotes economic growth through its Institute for Rural Vitality and an extensive network of community, statewide, and national partners. We actively strengthen the industries that align with our academic programs and mission by providing modern business resources, training, and technical assistance while offering shared-use facilities that support regional entrepreneurs, creating new connections for sustainable development. Drawing on a rich academic tradition that spans more than 100 years, our purpose remains constant: to educate and support undergraduate learners and researchers to be workforce-ready and affect the two most significant forces in today's world - agriculture and technology. Job Description: The Assistant Registrar reports directly to the Registrar. This position is one of two Assistant Registrars in the Office of the Registrar. This individual will serve as a SUNY Reconnect Navigator and Credit for Prior Learning Coordinator. This individual will also maintain and update curriculum revisions/additions, and aid in the administration of system operations to ensure accuracy of student records and reporting of those records. This position works closely with staff, faculty, and students regarding curriculum, college catalog, grades, student standing, master schedule, student degree audits, and records retention. The Assistant Registrar is expected to have strong functional knowledge of institutional processes and procedures and how to translate these for inclusion into technological tools which support degree completion. Evening and weekend hours may be required. Duties include but not limited to: Serve as a campus SUNY Reconnect Navigator providing information, support, and assistance regarding services and academic progress to adult learners. Serve of Credit for Prior Learning Coordinator. Maintain and update curriculum revisions/additions in Banner and the College Catalog. Scribe degree programs and curricular changes in and maintain Degree Works. Assist with the review of degree applicants for conferral of degree. Review and process dual degree/second degree applications. Review and process Cross Registration applications. Conduct training for faculty, staff, and students on use of Banner Self Service and Degree Works tools. Support initiatives to bring new students to campus by aiding in the registration of students and addressing concerns of all incoming students. Coordinate and communicate with advisors concerning advisees and meeting their degree completion goals. Requirements: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. If you currently need sponsorship or will need it in the future to maintain employment authorization, you do not meet eligibility requirements. Additionally, please note that SUNY Cobleskill is not an E-Verify employer. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's Degree from an accredited college or university. At least three years of experience working in a Registrar's office, or comparable, setting. Excellent organization, administration, communication, and decision-making skills. Must be able to work independently, as well as within a team, in a fast-paced, continually changing environment. A working knowledge of FERPA. Preferred Qualifications In addition to the aforementioned minimum qualifications, it is preferred the candidate also have: Master's degree from an accredited college or university. Strong understanding of Banner {Student Information System) and Degree Works (Degree Auditing System) including Controller. Experience with business intelligence systems. An understanding of programming for data query from information systems and the languages used. Experience with data collection and data analysis for informed decision making and problem solving. Additional Information: The salary range for this position is $57,151 - $65,000. This is a full-time, term appointment. This position offers full New York State benefits which are some of the most comprehensive in the country. SUNY Cobleskill is committed to providing a safe and productive learning and living community for our students and employees. To achieve that goal, we conduct background investigations for all final candidates being considered for employment. Any offer of employment is contingent on the successful completion of the background check. This position may be designated 55 b/c and is subject to verification of applicant eligibility. All people with disabilities are encouraged to apply, if your skillset meets the minimum requirements as set forth. SUNY Cobleskill is committed to fostering a diverse community of outstanding faculty, staff, and students, and ensuring equal educational and employment opportunity and access to services, programs and activities without regard to an individual's race, color, national origin, religion, creed, age, disability, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, familial status, pregnancy, predisposing genetic characteristics, military status, domestic violence victim status, criminal conviction, or any other legally protected status.  Individuals with disabilities requiring accommodation during the hiring process should contact the Human Resources Office at (518) 255-5514. SUNY Cobleskill's Interim Title IX Coordinator is Nicole Field, who can be contacted via phone at 518-255-5514, email at fieldnm@cobleskill.edu, or mailing address of SUNY Cobleskill,  Knapp Hall Room 123, Cobleskill, NY 12043. If you have any questions regarding this position, please contact humanresources@cobleskill.edu or 518-255-5423. Application Instructions: The application review process will begin immediately and will continue until a suitable candidate is found. Priority is given to applications submitted by October 8, 2026. A complete application consists of the following: 1. Letter of application, addressing qualifications, position responsibilities, and college values 2. Current CV or Resume 3. Names, emails and phone numbers of at least three professional references, preferably former supervisors * To apply you must create an account and apply through this posting. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation SUNY Cobleskill 106 Suffolk Circle, Cobleskill, NY 12043"
    },
    "https://cobleskill.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205321": {
      "title": "SUNY Cobleskill Ag & Tech Institute for Rural Vitality - Marketing Communications Specialist",
      "description": "SUNY Cobleskill\nJob Posting: SUNY Cobleskill Ag & Tech Institute for Rural Vitality - Marketing Communications Specialist SUNY Cobleskill \n\tJob Posting: SUNY Cobleskill Ag & Tech Institute for Rural Vitality - Marketing Communications Specialist Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details SUNY Cobleskill Ag & Tech Institute for Rural Vitality - Marketing Communications Specialist Category: Staff Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Research Foundation Locations: Cobleskill, NY Posted: Sep 29, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Temporary Salary Type: Annually Salary Range: $58,000.00 - $65,000.00 Position ID: 205321 Share About SUNY Cobleskill: The State University of New York College of Agriculture and Technology at Cobleskill is an accredited polytechnic college that is primarily baccalaureate and residential. SUNY Cobleskill is one of few institutions in the United States where an undergraduate student may also choose to earn an associate degree. The College has an enrollment of approximately 2,500 students in 59 programs divided into two schools, Agriculture and Natural Resources; Business and Liberal Arts & Sciences. An economic engine for rural New York, SUNY Cobleskill is home to a wide array of extended learning and professional development opportunities. Modeling experiential education, SUNY Cobleskill is committed to providing small classes, excellent facilities, and a hands-on learning environment. Whether students plan to further their education or begin their professional careers, they graduate with the foundation to pursue their goals and to succeed. Job Description: Employment is with The Research Foundation for The State University of New York, a private, nonprofit, educational corporation that administers sponsored program activity for SUNY Cobleskill. SUNY Cobleskill seeks a Marketing Communications Specialist (MCS) who will be responsible for marketing plan preparation, creating content, and implementing an integrated marketing communications and brand strategy across a variety of channels, including digital and print. The MCS will lead efforts in promoting USDA programs and creating promotional materials to reach current and prospective participants for the Institute for Rural Vitality (IRV). The successful candidate will also provide recruitment and support to underserved farmers, ranchers and veterans on behalf of the Office of Partnerships and Public Engagement (OPPE). Requirements: Bachelor's degree in Marketing, Communications, Business, Agriculture, or related field from an accredited institution. Proficiency in digital marketing tools, social media platforms, and design software (e.g., Canva, Adobe Creative Suite). Exceptional organizational, project and event management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines. Strong interpersonal skills and ability to engage effectively with government representatives, stakeholders, and community members. Familiarity with SUNY Cobleskill's and the Institute for Rural Vitality's missions, visions and programming. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, including Outlook for email management Ability to build relationships, alliances, and partnerships with internal and external organizations. Ability to travel to off-campus events and programs Ability to work as part of a team. Preferred Qualifications: Graduate degree in marketing, business, agricultural business management, or a related program.Strong knowledge of USDA initiatives, grant opportunities, and rural/agricultural development programs. 5+ years of professional experience in marketing and communications; experience with USDA programs Experience in grant administration preferred. Project management skills preferred. Understanding and/or demonstrated familiarity with small business operations. In-depth knowledge of working with veterans, farmers, and/or ranchers Agriculture industry experience is preferred. Additional Information: $58,000 - $65,000 Monday-Friday 8:30- 4:30 PM. Occasional nights and weekends Full-time October 15, 2026 - September 29, 2028 Renewal contingent on additional funding This is a Research Foundation position. Application Instructions: Persons interested in the above position should submit a resume, three work-related references, along with a letter of application. The Research Foundation for The State University of New York, an EEO/AA/IRCA/ADA employer, offers exceptional benefits such as healthcare, dental, vision, pension plans, competitive pay, generous paid time off, tuition assistance, life insurance and long-term disability insurance. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation SUNY Cobleskill 106 Suffolk Circle, Cobleskill, NY 12043"
    },
    "https://cobleskill.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205479": {
      "title": "Advocate",
      "description": "SUNY Cobleskill\nJob Posting: Advocate - SUNY Cobleskill Liberty Partnership Program for Research Foundation SUNY Cobleskill \n\tJob Posting: Advocate - SUNY Cobleskill Liberty Partnership Program for Research Foundation Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Advocate - SUNY Cobleskill Liberty Partnership Program for Research Foundation Category: Staff Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Research Foundation Locations: Cobleskill, NY Posted: Oct 5, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Temporary Salary Type: Annually Salary Range: $58,000.00 - $65,000.00 Position ID: 205479 Share About SUNY Cobleskill: The State University of New York College of Agriculture and Technology at Cobleskill is an accredited polytechnic college that is primarily baccalaureate and residential. SUNY Cobleskill is one of few institutions in the United States where an undergraduate student may also choose to earn an associate degree. The College has an enrollment of approximately 2,500 students in 59 programs divided into two schools, Agriculture and Natural Resources; Business and Liberal Arts & Sciences. An economic engine for rural New York, SUNY Cobleskill is home to a wide array of extended learning and professional development opportunities. Modeling experiential education, SUNY Cobleskill is committed to providing small classes, excellent facilities, and a hands-on learning environment. Whether students plan to further their education or begin their professional careers, they graduate with the foundation to pursue their goals and to succeed. Job Description: This position is offered through the SUNY Research Foundation, a private not-for-profit organization located on the SUNY Cobleskill campus. The position is physically located at Schoharie Central Jr/Sr High School in Schoharie, NY. Employment is through the SUNY Research Foundation and is not affiliated with the State of New York or the State University of New York. The SUNY Cobleskill Liberty Partnerships Program (LPP) is a New York State Education Department-funded dropout prevention program serving students in grades 6-12 and their families across seven school districts in Schoharie, Otsego, and Delaware Counties. LPP works to support students in graduating from high school and successfully transitioning to post-secondary education and/or the workforce. This Advocate position will be located at Schoharie Central Jr/Sr High School and will provide services to LPP students in the areas of academic support, social-emotional development, life skills, goal setting, mentoring, leadership, service learning, and college and career readiness. The Advocate will represent LPP within the school and district, collaborate with school and LPP staff, and advocate for LPP students. Services may be provided individually or in small groups during the school day and through field trips, workshops, and occasional after-school or evening activities. The Advocate will engage parents/guardians, connect students and families with appropriate resources, follow mandated reporter requirements and SUNY Cobleskill Child Protection Policies, and maintain confidentiality. Responsibilities include: Developing and maintaining personal learning plans and social-emotional assessments with students Maintaining accurate records of student and family contacts and submitting required data in a timely manner Coordinating services with LPP and district staff Participating in professional development Completing other duties necessary to meet the goals of LPP and the needs of students and families The Advocate will report directly to the LPP Project Director. Desired skills and strengths: Strong interpersonal and communication skills; empathy, patience, dependability, and respect for others; flexibility and adaptability; ability to work collaboratively and independently; honesty; organization; and attention to detail. Research Foundation for SUNY offers exceptional benefits such as healthcare, dental, vision, pension plans, competitive pay, generous paid time off, tuition assistance, life insurance and long-term disability insurance. As an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action employer, Research Foundation for SUNY at SUNY Cobleskill will not discriminate in its employment practices due to an applicant's race, creed, religion, color, citizenship, national origin, sex, age, sexual orientation, predisposing genetic characteristics, gender identification or expression, genetic information, familial status, marital status, pregnancy, status as a domestic violence victim, recently separated veteran, or other characteristics as protected by law. Please feel free to review your equal employment opportunities protections and laws pertaining to these protections at http://www.dol.gov/ofccp/regs/compliance/posters/pdf/eeopost.pdf. Requirements: Bachelor's Degree 1+ years of experience working with adolescents Preferred Qualifications: Master's Degree 1+ years of experience working with adolescents in a school setting Additional Information: Hourly Rate: $17.30/hour Part-Time: 30-35 hours/week Annual Length of Assignment: 10-months (September-June) Position Location: Schoharie Jr/Sr High School, Schoharie, NY The application review process will begin immediately and will continue until a suitable candidate is found. 1. A resume outlining education and work experience 2. A cover letter stating how your education and work experience relates to the job responsibilities outlined above 3. Three professional references All applications must be submitted online at https://www.cobleskill.edu/about/offices-services/human-resources/index.aspx under Current Openings. Application Instructions: Persons interested in the above position should submit a resume, three work-related references, along with a letter of application. The Research Foundation for The State University of New York, an EEO/AA/IRCA/ADA employer, offers exceptional benefits such as healthcare, dental, vision, pension plans, competitive pay, generous paid time off, tuition assistance, life insurance and long-term disability insurance. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation SUNY Cobleskill 106 Suffolk Circle, Cobleskill, NY 12043"
    },
    "https://cobleskill.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205753": {
      "title": "CAS - #680 - Utility, Union, Full-Time, Non-Exempt",
      "description": "SUNY Cobleskill\nJob Posting: CAS - #680 - Utility, Union, Full-Time, Non-Exempt SUNY Cobleskill \n\tJob Posting: CAS - #680 - Utility, Union, Full-Time, Non-Exempt Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details CAS - #680 - Utility, Union, Full-Time, Non-Exempt Category: CAS Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: CAS Locations: Cobleskill, NY Posted: Oct 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 205753 Share About SUNY Cobleskill: SUNY Cobleskill Auxiliary Services, (CAS) is a not-for-profit corporation committed to delivering quality services at competitive prices to the SUNY Cobleskill campus community.  CAS Provides outstanding services that collectively support students, faculty, staff, and guests while contributing to the local community.  Through expert customer service, CAS supports the pursuit of academic excellence, encourages personal growth, and cultivates sustainability, yielding superior quality of life for all. Job Description: Position requires the preparation and service of food and beverages. Requires the use and operation of food service/kitchen equipment and appliances. Operate point-of-sale system and follow all cashiering guidelines Perform duties as outlined in the Standard Operating Procedures. Must be able to read Possess and utilize organizational qualities Self-Starter Free of communicable diseases and protection of all open cuts, wounds, and skin abrasions ServSafe® Certified within one-year of hire Professional, neat, and clean appearance, wearing slip-resistant footwear and full uniform Respectful of co-workers and customers Must be customer-service minded with an emphasis on quality Must be able to work in a diverse environment. PRIMARY DUTIES: Utility/dishroom, evening floor cleaner for all of Champlin and Coby's. ADDITIONAL WORK-RELATED INFORMATION: You may be called upon, at times, to work at related tasks, within your classification, not included in the above description. You may be required to temporarily work in a lower classification without change in pay rate. You may be requested to temporarily work on a job in a higher classification at your existing pay rate, however, you may refuse without fear of management condemnation. You may be scheduled to work at special functions, picnics, etc. held on- or off-campus, and to perform duties related to the above description. Work schedules, days off, job assignments, and unit assignments are subject to change and/or adjustment based upon the needs of the operation. Occasional evening and weekend work may be required, as job duties fluctuate. Working Conditions: This job operates in a professional kitchen environment with many types of equipment. Physical Demands: The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully and safely perform the essential functions of this job. May be required to lift up to 50 pounds May be required to be on feet for duration of shift May be required to sit for duration of shift. Some bending and stretching may be required to place items on low and high shelves Possible exposure to extreme temperatures, slippery floors, and high humidity Work with some chemical cleaning agents The employee is frequently required to stand, walk, use hands and fingers to handle and feel, reach with hands and arms, communicate/give and receive information effectively. ADDITIONAL WORK-RELATED INFORMATION: You may be called upon, at times, to work at related tasks, within your classification, not included in the above description. You may be required to temporarily work in a lower classification without change in pay rate. You may be requested to temporarily work on a job in a higher classification at your existing pay rate, however, you may refuse without fear of management condemnation. You may be scheduled to work at special functions, picnics, etc. held on- or off-campus, and to perform duties related to the above description. Work schedules, days off, job assignments, and unit assignments are subject to change and/or adjustment based upon the needs of the operation. Occasional evening and weekend work may be required, as job duties fluctuate. OTHER DUTIES Please note:  This job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties, or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job.  Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time, with or without notice, based upon the needs of the operation. Requirements: High School Diploma or equivalent Additional Information: Salary: $16.50/hour Full-time, Non-Exempt, Union, 32 weeks/year (coincides with Academic Calendar) 35 hours/week 4:30 pm - 12:00 am, Sunday - Thursday EEO STATEMENT: EEO employees are protected by federal laws, Presidential Executive Orders and other laws from discrimination on the basis of race, religion, color, sex (including pregnancy and gender identity), sexual orientation, parental status, national origin, age, disability, family medical history or genetic information, political affiliations, military service or any other non-merit-based factors.  These protections extend to all management practices and decisions, including recruitment and hiring practices, appraisal systems, promotions, and training and career development programs.  EEO employees are also protected against retaliation.  Consistent with federal laws, acts of retaliation against an employee who engages in a protected activity, whistle blowing, or the exercise of any appeal or grievance right provided by law, will not be tolerated.  In addition, consistent with the RESOLVED Program (Non-Retaliation Statement9, the EEOC will not tolerate retaliation against participation in RESOLVE. EEOC managers and supervisors are also reminded again of their responsibility to prevent, document and promptly address/correct harassing conduct in the workplace. Application Instructions: The application review process will begin immediately and will continue until a suitable candidate is found. Please provide: Resume Cover Letter References Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation SUNY Cobleskill 106 Suffolk Circle, Cobleskill, NY 12043"
    },
    "https://cobleskill.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205671": {
      "title": "CAS - Sous Chef, Full-Time, Non-Union, Non-Exempt",
      "description": "SUNY Cobleskill\nJob Posting: CAS - Sous Chef, Full-Time, Non-Union, Non-Exempt SUNY Cobleskill \n\tJob Posting: CAS - Sous Chef, Full-Time, Non-Union, Non-Exempt Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details CAS - Sous Chef, Full-Time, Non-Union, Non-Exempt Category: CAS Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: CAS Locations: Cobleskill, NY Posted: Oct 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 205671 Share About SUNY Cobleskill: SUNY Cobleskill Auxiliary Services, (CAS) is a not-for-profit corporation committed to delivering quality services at competitive prices to the SUNY Cobleskill campus community.  CAS Provides outstanding services that collectively support students, faculty, staff, and guests while contributing to the local community.  Through expert customer service, CAS supports the pursuit of academic excellence, encourages personal growth, and cultivates sustainability, yielding superior quality of life for all. Job Description: The Sous Chef is responsible for supporting the daily culinary operations of the operation while maintaining high standards for food quality, food safety, sanitation, production, and customer satisfaction. This position provides hands-on leadership to culinary staff and assists with menu execution, production planning, HACCP compliance, allergen management, inventory control, food rotation, waste reduction, and staff training. The Sous Chef is expected to lead by example and help create a professional, organized, safe, and positive kitchen environment. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Culinary Operations & Production Assist in overseeing daily breakfast, lunch, dinner, catering, and special-event production as assigned. Ensure recipes, menus, portion standards, and production specifications are followed consistently. Review production needs based on meal forecasts, historical counts, events, and anticipated customer volume. Develop and communicate daily prep and production assignments. Conduct pre-service line checks to ensure food quality, presentation, temperature, and station readiness. Taste food throughout production and service to ensure consistency and quality. Ensure food is prepared and served on time. Assist with special menus, theme meals, pop-ups, catering events, and culinary programs. Work directly with culinary staff during high-volume meal periods as needed. HACCP, Food Safety & Sanitation Ensure all food is prepared, cooked, cooled, reheated, held, and stored according to established HACCP procedures and applicable food safety requirements. Monitor and verify food temperatures throughout production and service. Ensure required HACCP and temperature logs are completed accurately and on time. Maintain proper labeling, dating, storage, and food rotation using FIFO principles. Monitor walk-in coolers, freezers, dry storage, and production areas for proper organization and sanitation. Ensure raw and ready-to-eat foods are stored correctly to prevent cross-contamination. Maintain high standards for personal hygiene, handwashing, glove use, and proper utensil handling. Correct food safety concerns immediately and communicate significant issues to culinary leadership. Participate in routine kitchen, cooler, freezer, and storage-area inspections. Allergen Management Ensure staff understand and follow established allergen procedures. Verify allergen-sensitive meals are prepared using appropriate equipment, utensils, ingredients, and procedures. Help prevent cross-contact during preparation, storage, cooking, and service. Verify ingredient and recipe information when responding to allergen-related questions. Inventory, Receiving & Food Rotation Assist with inventory counts and maintaining appropriate par levels. Maintain FIFO rotation throughout coolers, freezers, dry storage, and production areas. Identify short-dated products and develop appropriate production plans to utilize them safely. Waste Reduction & Cost Control Monitor production quantities and leftovers to reduce food waste. Utilize production records and meal counts to improve future forecasting. Ensure portion-control standards are followed. Identify opportunities to safely repurpose appropriate ingredients while maintaining quality and food safety standards. Support departmental food-cost and sustainability goals. Staff Leadership & Training Provide daily direction and supervision to cooks, food service workers, and other assigned staff. Conduct or participate in daily culinary huddles before service. Clearly communicate production expectations, priorities, menu changes, and food safety requirements. Train staff on recipes, cooking techniques, equipment use, HACCP, sanitation, allergens, labeling, food rotation, and portion control. Hold staff accountable for established culinary and food safety standards. Promote teamwork, professionalism, respect, and effective communication. Assist culinary leadership with identifying training and development needs. Provide constructive coaching and immediately address operational concerns. Equipment & Kitchen Organization Ensure kitchen equipment is properly operated, cleaned, and maintained. Report damaged or malfunctioning equipment promptly. Maintain clean, organized, and professional work areas. Ensure knives, smallwares, utensils, and equipment are properly stored. Support opening and closing procedures and verify assigned tasks are completed before staff leave. CUSTOMER EXPERIENCE Maintain a customer-focused approach to dining services. Interact professionally with guests, staff, visitors, and other partners. Respond appropriately to customer feedback and communicate concerns to management. Support culinary innovation and menu variety, including plant-forward, vegan, vegetarian, culturally diverse, and allergen-conscious offerings. Help create a dining environment that supports customer satisfaction and the overall dining experience. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS Ability to stand and walk for extended periods. Ability to lift and carry up to 50 pounds as required. Ability to work around hot equipment, steam, refrigeration, freezers, and wet surfaces. Ability to bend, reach, push, pull, and perform repetitive kitchen tasks. Ability to safely operate commercial food-service equipment. SCHEDULING REQUIREMENTS Dining operations may operate seven days a week. The Sous Chef must have scheduling flexibility based on operational needs and may be required to work mornings, evenings, weekends, holidays, special events, and functions. PERFORMANCE EXPECTATIONS Success in this position will be measured by consistent food quality, customer satisfaction, effective production planning, HACCP compliance, allergen safety, proper labeling and rotation, sanitation standards, waste reduction, inventory control, staff development, teamwork, and overall operational execution. The Sous Chef is expected to be a visible, hands-on culinary leader who takes ownership of the operation and consistently supports the standards and goals of Dining Services. Requirements: QUALIFICATIONS Culinary degree or equivalent professional culinary experience preferred. Minimum 3-5 years of professional culinary experience, preferably in high-volume food service. Previous supervisory or leadership experience preferred. High-volume dining, healthcare, hospitality, institutional dining, or large-volume food service experience strongly preferred. Ability to read and execute standardized recipes. Experience with production planning, forecasting, inventory, and portion control. Strong organizational, communication, and leadership skills. Ability to work effectively in a fast-paced, high-volume environment. ServSafe Manager certification or equivalent food safety certification preferred or required within an established period after hire. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS Ability to stand and walk for extended periods. Ability to lift and carry up to 50 pounds as required. Ability to work around hot equipment, steam, refrigeration, freezers, and wet surfaces. Ability to bend, reach, push, pull, and perform repetitive kitchen tasks. Ability to safely operate commercial food-service equipment. Additional Information: Employment Status: Full-Time, Variable Schedule Based on Operational Needs Rate: Based on experience $26.00 - $28.00/hour, Non-Exempt EEO STATEMENT: EEO employees are protected by federal laws, Presidential Executive Orders and other laws from discrimination on the basis of race, religion, color, sex (including pregnancy and gender identity), sexual orientation, parental status, national origin, age, disability, family medical history or genetic information, political affiliations, military service or any other non-merit-based factors.  These protections extend to all management practices and decisions, including recruitment and hiring practices, appraisal systems, promotions, and training and career development programs.  EEO employees are also protected against retaliation.  Consistent with federal laws, acts of retaliation against an employee who engages in a protected activity, whistle blowing, or the exercise of any appeal or grievance right provided by law, will not be tolerated.  In addition, consistent with the RESOLVED Program (Non-Retaliation Statement9, the EEOC will not tolerate retaliation against participation in RESOLVE. EEOC managers and supervisors are also reminded again of their responsibility to prevent, document and promptly address/correct harassing conduct in the workplace. Application Instructions: The application review process will begin immediately and will continue until a suitable candidate is found. Please provide: Resume Cover Letter References Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation SUNY Cobleskill 106 Suffolk Circle, Cobleskill, NY 12043"
    },
    "https://cobleskill.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205667": {
      "title": "CAS - Purchasing Agent, FT, Non-Union, Non-Exempt",
      "description": "SUNY Cobleskill\nJob Posting: CAS - Purchasing Agent, FT, Non-Union, Non-Exempt SUNY Cobleskill \n\tJob Posting: CAS - Purchasing Agent, FT, Non-Union, Non-Exempt Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details CAS - Purchasing Agent, FT, Non-Union, Non-Exempt Category: CAS Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: CAS Locations: Cobleskill, NY Posted: Oct 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 205667 Share About SUNY Cobleskill: SUNY Cobleskill Auxiliary Services, (CAS) is a not-for-profit corporation committed to delivering quality services at competitive prices to the SUNY Cobleskill campus community.  CAS Provides outstanding services that collectively support students, faculty, staff, and guests while contributing to the local community.  Through expert customer service, CAS supports the pursuit of academic excellence, encourages personal growth, and cultivates sustainability, yielding superior quality of life for all. Job Description: The Purchasing Agent is responsible for purchasing, inventory control, proper storage, and product rotation for the dining operation. This position works closely with Senior Dining Director, Director of Culinary Strategy and Innovation, culinary team, management, vendors and receiving personnel to ensure the correct products and quantities are available for forecasted service while maintaining fiscal responsibility, food quality, HACCP compliance, and safe food-handling practices. VENDOR MANAGEMENT Maintain an accurate vendor list including vendor name, approved products, account information, contact information, ordering procedures and delivery days. Maintain current ordering information and approved order guides for each vendor for both Retail and Residential Dining Director's. Communicate product shortages, substitutions, delivery issues, and pricing concerns to culinary leadership. Verify that products received meet established specifications, quality standards, and food-safety requirements. Maintain professional relationships with vendors and promptly communicate shortages, substitutions, credits, damaged products, delivery issues, pricing discrepancies and food-safety concerns. ORDER GUIDE MANAGEMENT Create and maintain organized order guides for each approved vendor. Organize order guides by storage area, including dry storage, refrigerated storage, frozen storage, and front-of-house storage using shelf to sheet process. Products should be listed in the sequence in which they are physically stored whenever possible to improve ordering, inventory, rotation and receiving efficiency. Ensure the correct approved products, pack sizes, and specifications are listed. Review order guides regularly and remove discontinued, duplicated, or unnecessary items. ORDERING Review menus, recipes, production forecasts, catering needs, and special events before placing orders.  This should be in conjunction with the Executive Chef & Catering Manager. Check inventory, on hand, before ordering to prevent unnecessary purchases and overstocking. Use established par levels and actual usage to determine order quantities. Coordinate with chefs and managers regarding menu changes, anticipated volume, substitutions, and special purchasing needs. Place orders within established deadlines and maintain appropriate documentation (PO's). Communicate EO-32 products added and used to Dining Director. RECEIVING & HACCP COMPLIANCE Verify all deliveries against purchase orders and invoices for correct product, quantity, quality, and condition. Inspect products for damage, contamination, signs of thawing, spoilage, or compromised packaging. Check and document required receiving temperatures for refrigerated and frozen foods according to the operation's HACCP procedures. Document and Reject products that do not meet food-safety, temperature, quality, or purchasing specifications. Ensure receiving areas are kept clean, organized, and protected from cross-contamination. STORAGE & FOOD ROTATION Ensure all products are stored promptly in the correct designated storage area. Maintain strict FIFO (First In, First Out) rotation throughout dry, refrigerated, and frozen storage. Place older usable products in front and newer products behind them. Keep food off the floor and maintain proper separation from chemicals and non-food items. Maintain organized storage areas so inventory can be easily identified, counted, and rotated. Immediately report expired, damaged, spoiled, or improperly stored products to management. REFRIGERATED & FROZEN STORAGE Monitor refrigerated and frozen storage areas for proper organization and food-safety practices. Verify products are maintained at safe temperatures and stored to prevent cross-contamination. Ensure raw animal products are stored according to proper food-safety hierarchy. Prevent unnecessary freezer inventory and excessive stock that may contribute to waste, poor rotation, or loss of quality. Review frozen products regularly for freezer damage, age, and unnecessary accumulation. DATING AND LABELING Ensure all applicable products are properly labeled, dated, and identifiable. Verify opened, prepared, and transferred products follow established dating and labeling procedures. Remove or report products with missing, incorrect, illegible, or expired labels. Support consistent labeling practices throughout all storage areas. DISTRIBUTION Coordinate product distribution to production areas based on menu needs, forecasts, and approved requisitions. Ensure products issued from storage are properly rotated and older products are used first. Maintain accountability for products moving between storage and production areas. WEEKLY INVENTORY & ROTATION AUDIT Conduct routine audits of dry storage, coolers, and freezers. Check for expired products, short-dated products, improper rotation, missing labels, overstocking, and food-safety concerns. Communicate findings to culinary leadership and assist with corrective action. Identify short-dated products early so culinary staff can safely incorporate them into menus when appropriate. FOOD WASTE & INVENTORY CONTROL Support inventory levels that meet operational needs without creating excessive stock.  Adjust ordering quantities and par levels based on actual usage, forecasts, delivery frequency, and operational needs. Monitor high-cost and high-usage items for unusual purchasing or usage patterns. Work with culinary leadership, including receivers, Director of Culinary Strategy and Innovation, and the Dining Director, to reduce waste caused by over-ordering, poor rotation, expired products, and improper storage. Participate in physical inventory counts and assist with reconciling inventory discrepancies. HACCP & FOOD-SAFETY RESPONSIBILITIES Follow all established HACCP procedures related to receiving, storage, temperature control, sanitation, and cross-contamination prevention. Maintain required receiving and temperature documentation accurately and consistently. Report food-safety concerns immediately to management. Support corrective actions when temperatures, storage conditions, labeling, or rotation do not meet established standards. Maintain clean, sanitary, and inspection-ready storage and receiving areas. PERFORMANCE EXPECTATIONS Orders are accurate, timely, and based on operational need. Inventory is organized, properly rotated, labeled, and maintained at appropriate levels. Receiving and HACCP documentation is completed consistently. Expired products, excessive inventory, and avoidable food waste are minimized. Storage areas remain clean, safe, organized, and inspection ready. ACCOUNTABILITY The Purchasing Agent is accountable for maintaining purchasing and inventory practices that support food safety, operational efficiency, fiscal responsibility, and product quality. Repeated failures in receiving controls, HACCP documentation, labeling, rotation, inventory accuracy, or established purchasing procedures must be reported and corrected in coordination with culinary leadership. Requirements: REQUIRED SKILLS AND QUALIFICATION S Experience in food-service purchasing, receiving, inventory, culinary operations, or a related field preferred. Knowledge of food safety, HACCP principles, FIFO rotation, receiving temperatures, temperature control, proper food storage, dating and labeling, and inventory management. Strong organizational, communication, and recordkeeping skills. Ability to analyze inventory levels, usage, par levels, and purchasing needs. Ability to work collaboratively with chefs, managers, directors, vendors, and receiving personnel. Ability to safely lift and move products as required by the operation. Food-Safety certification such as ServSafe or equivalent is strongly preferred, must take within 4 months of hire. Additional Information: Hours:  Monday-Friday: 7:00 am - 3:00 pm Saturday & Sunday: Off (May Be Required to Work Based on Business Needs/Large Events) Hourly, Non-Exempt, 37.5 hours/week, (coincides with the academic calendar) 40-weeks per year Rate: Based on experience $24.00 - $28.00/hour EEO STATEMENT: EEO employees are protected by federal laws, Presidential Executive Orders and other laws from discrimination on the basis of race, religion, color, sex (including pregnancy and gender identity), sexual orientation, parental status, national origin, age, disability, family medical history or genetic information, political affiliations, military service or any other non-merit-based factors.  These protections extend to all management practices and decisions, including recruitment and hiring practices, appraisal systems, promotions, and training and career development programs.  EEO employees are also protected against retaliation.  Consistent with federal laws, acts of retaliation against an employee who engages in a protected activity, whistle blowing, or the exercise of any appeal or grievance right provided by law, will not be tolerated.  In addition, consistent with the RESOLVED Program (Non-Retaliation Statement9, the EEOC will not tolerate retaliation against participation in RESOLVE. EEOC managers and supervisors are also reminded again of their responsibility to prevent, document and promptly address/correct harassing conduct in the workplace. Application Instructions: The application review process will begin immediately and will continue until a suitable candidate is found. Please provide: Resume Cover Letter References Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation SUNY Cobleskill 106 Suffolk Circle, Cobleskill, NY 12043"
